          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When someone says they have heart failure, does that mean the heart has stopped working?",
          "answer": "No, not in the sense of the heart stopping. The name \"heart failure\" is misleading. A heart that has stopped would cause immediate collapse. Heart failure is a long-term condition, often managed for many years, in which the heart cannot fill with or pump out enough blood to meet the body's needs. The heart is still working, but not well enough. (Source: General clinical reference, \"Heart failure\".)\n\nDoctors often describe it using the ejection fraction. This is the share of blood in the heart's main pumping chamber that is pushed out with each beat. The groups are:\n\n- **Reduced**: 40 percent or below. The heart muscle contracts weakly.\n- **Mildly reduced**: 41 to 49 percent.\n- **Preserved**: 50 percent or above. The heart squeezes normally, but it stiffens and does not fill well between beats. This is not a milder form. It is a different problem, and it makes up roughly half of cases. (Sources: General clinical reference, \"Heart failure\"; Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction\".)\n\nThe group matters because treatment advice differs between them.\n\nIf someone is short of breath, has chest pain, or has sudden severe symptoms, they should seek emergency care right away. Anyone with questions about their own heart should talk with their clinician, who can explain what their results mean.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
